WEBVTT

00:00.570 --> 00:02.450
- [Narrator] When we first
learned algebra together,

00:02.450 --> 00:06.160
we started factoring polynomials,
especially quadratics.

00:06.160 --> 00:09.810
We recognized that an
expression like x squared

00:09.810 --> 00:12.920
could be written as x times x.

00:12.920 --> 00:17.920
We also recognized that a
polynomial like three x squared

00:18.600 --> 00:22.930
plus four x, that in
this situation both terms

00:22.930 --> 00:24.440
have the common factor of x

00:24.440 --> 00:25.860
and you could factor that out

00:25.860 --> 00:27.540
and so you could rewrite this as

00:27.540 --> 00:31.750
x times three x plus four.

00:31.750 --> 00:34.300
And we also learned to do fancier things.

00:34.300 --> 00:36.260
We learned to factor things like

00:36.260 --> 00:40.610
x squared plus seven x plus 12.

00:40.610 --> 00:43.050
We were able to say, "Hey!
What two numbers would add up

00:43.050 --> 00:44.820
to seven, and if I were to multiply them

00:44.820 --> 00:47.040
I'd get 12, and in those early videos,

00:47.040 --> 00:48.400
we show why that worked, and we'd say

00:48.400 --> 00:51.330
well, three and four, so
maybe this can be factored as

00:51.330 --> 00:56.330
x plus three times x plus four.

00:56.420 --> 00:58.590
If this is unfamiliar
to you, I encourage you

00:58.590 --> 00:59.910
to go review that in some of the

00:59.910 --> 01:03.300
introductory factoring
quadratics on Khan Academy.

01:03.300 --> 01:04.705
It should be review at this point

01:04.705 --> 01:06.910
in your journey.

01:06.910 --> 01:09.680
We also looked at things
like differences of squares.

01:09.680 --> 01:11.200
X squared minus nine.

01:11.200 --> 01:13.139
We'd say "Hey, that's x
squared minus three squared,

01:13.139 --> 01:16.330
so we could factor that as x plus three

01:16.330 --> 01:18.820
times x minus three. And
we looked at other types

01:18.820 --> 01:20.580
of quadratics.

01:20.580 --> 01:24.100
Now, as we go deeper into
our algebra journeys,

01:24.100 --> 01:25.830
we're going to build on this to

01:25.830 --> 01:29.010
factor higher degree polynomials.

01:29.010 --> 01:30.810
Third degree, fourth
degree, fifth degree, which

01:30.810 --> 01:33.980
will be very useful in
your mathematical careers.

01:33.980 --> 01:36.610
But we're going to start
doing it by really looking

01:36.610 --> 01:38.570
at some of the structures,
some of the patterns

01:38.570 --> 01:41.370
that we seen in introductory algebra.

01:41.370 --> 01:44.340
For example, let's say someone
walks up to you on the street

01:44.340 --> 01:46.845
and says, "Can you factor x to the third

01:46.845 --> 01:51.845
plus seven x squared plus 12 x?"

01:52.350 --> 01:54.461
Well, at first your wanna say
"Oh, this is a third-degree

01:54.461 --> 01:56.680
polynomial that seems
kind of intimidating",

01:56.680 --> 01:58.760
until you realize, "Hey,
all of these terms have

01:58.760 --> 02:01.830
the common factor x, so
if I factor that out,

02:01.830 --> 02:06.830
then it becomes x times x
squared plus seven x plus 12."

02:07.380 --> 02:10.160
And then, this is exactly
what we saw over here,

02:10.160 --> 02:12.170
so we could rewrite all of this as

02:12.170 --> 02:17.170
x times x plus three times x plus four.

02:17.600 --> 02:18.870
So we're going to see
that we might be able

02:18.870 --> 02:20.710
to do some simple factoring like this,

02:20.710 --> 02:23.210
and even factoring multiple times.

02:23.210 --> 02:26.220
We might also start to
appreciate structure

02:26.220 --> 02:27.290
that brings us back to

02:27.290 --> 02:30.100
some of what we saw in
our introductory algebra.

02:30.100 --> 02:35.060
So, for example, you might
see something like this,

02:35.060 --> 02:37.670
where, once again, someone
walks up to you on the street

02:37.670 --> 02:41.130
and says "Hey, you factor
this, a to the fourth power

02:41.130 --> 02:45.047
plus seven a squared plus
12," and at first you're like

02:45.047 --> 02:49.350
"Wow! There's a fourth
power here, what do I do?"

02:49.350 --> 02:52.970
Until you say "Well, what
if I were to rewrite this

02:52.970 --> 02:57.970
as a squared squared plus
seven a squared plus 12."

03:02.040 --> 03:06.160
And now, this a squared is
looking an awful lot like

03:06.160 --> 03:07.740
this x over here.

03:07.740 --> 03:11.060
If this were an x, than
this would be x squared.

03:11.060 --> 03:13.720
If this were an x, than
this would just be an x.

03:13.720 --> 03:16.160
And then these expressions
would be the same.

03:16.160 --> 03:18.600
So when I factor it,
everywhere I see an x,

03:18.600 --> 03:20.880
I could replace with an a squared.

03:20.880 --> 03:22.784
So I could factor this out
really looking at the same

03:22.784 --> 03:27.350
structure we have here
as a squared plus three

03:27.350 --> 03:29.820
times a squared plus four.

03:29.820 --> 03:31.920
Now, I'm going really
faster, this is really

03:31.920 --> 03:34.760
the introductory video,
the overview video.

03:34.760 --> 03:37.390
Don't worry if this is a
little bit much too fast.

03:37.390 --> 03:39.030
This is really just to
give you a sense of things.

03:39.030 --> 03:41.730
Later in this unit,
we're going to dig deeper

03:41.730 --> 03:44.130
into each of these cases.

03:44.130 --> 03:45.960
But just to give you a
sense of where we're going,

03:45.960 --> 03:48.510
I'll give you another example
that builds off of what you

03:48.510 --> 03:52.500
likely saw in your
introductory algebra learning.

03:52.500 --> 03:54.610
So, building off of the structure here,

03:54.610 --> 03:56.930
if someone were to walk up to you again,

03:56.930 --> 03:58.540
a lot of people are walking up to you,

03:58.540 --> 04:02.958
and say "Factor four x to the sixth

04:02.958 --> 04:06.600
minus nine y to the fourth."

04:06.600 --> 04:09.260
Well, at first, this
looks quite intimidating,

04:09.260 --> 04:11.750
until you realize that "Hey, I could write

04:11.750 --> 04:14.870
both of these as squares.
I could write this

04:14.870 --> 04:19.320
first one as two x to the third squared

04:19.320 --> 04:21.570
minus, and I could write this second term

04:21.570 --> 04:24.530
as three y squared squared."

04:24.530 --> 04:26.960
And now, this is just a
difference of squares.

04:26.960 --> 04:31.960
So it'd be, two x to the
third, plus three y squared

04:32.910 --> 04:37.910
times two x to the third
minus three y squared.

04:38.230 --> 04:40.010
We'll also see things like this where

04:40.010 --> 04:42.500
we're going to be
factoring multiple times.

04:42.500 --> 04:45.430
So, once again, someone
walks up to you in the street

04:45.430 --> 04:48.020
and they, you're a very popular person.

04:48.020 --> 04:50.227
Someone walks up to you
on the street and says

04:50.227 --> 04:53.820
"Factor x to the fourth
minus y to the fourth."

04:53.820 --> 04:55.280
Well, based on what we just saw,

04:55.280 --> 04:57.460
you could realize that
this is the same thing as

04:57.460 --> 05:02.200
x squared squared minus y squared squared,

05:02.200 --> 05:04.160
and you say "Okay, this is
a difference of squares,

05:04.160 --> 05:06.360
just like this was a
difference of squares."

05:06.360 --> 05:09.770
So it's going to be the sum of x squared

05:09.770 --> 05:12.870
and y squared, x squared plus y squared,

05:12.870 --> 05:14.370
times the difference of them.

05:14.370 --> 05:16.460
X squared minus y squared.

05:16.460 --> 05:19.660
Now this is fun, because this
too a difference of squares.

05:19.660 --> 05:21.600
So we can rewrite this whole thing as,

05:21.600 --> 05:25.410
I'll rewrite this first part, x squared,

05:25.410 --> 05:28.096
x squared plus y squared,
and then we can factor

05:28.096 --> 05:29.590
this as a difference of squares,

05:29.590 --> 05:31.360
just as we factored this up here.

05:31.360 --> 05:35.200
And we get x plus y times x minus y.

05:35.200 --> 05:36.360
So I'll leave you there.

05:36.360 --> 05:38.940
I've just bombarded you
with a bunch of information,

05:38.940 --> 05:41.170
but this is really just
to get you warmed up.

05:41.170 --> 05:43.281
Don't stress about it
because we're gonna go deep

05:43.281 --> 05:45.810
into each of these and there's
gonna be plenty of chances

05:45.810 --> 05:48.830
to practice it on Khan Academy
to make sure you understand

05:48.830 --> 05:51.430
where all of this is coming from.

05:51.430 --> 05:52.403
Enjoy.
